-- title:
--   Riemann–Hurwitz formula for tame separable extensions
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field, $F$ a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, and $F'$ a field that is an algebra over both $K$ and $F$, the two structures being compatible in the scalar-tower sense. Assume that $F$ is a function field of one variable over $K$ in the sense that some $x \in F$ is transcendental over $K$ and $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$; assume further that $F'$ is finite-dimensional and separable over $F$. Places of a field $L$ over $K$ are, by definition, valuation subrings of $L$ that contain the image of $K$, are proper, and are principal ideal rings; for a place $w$ of $F'$ over $K$, the ramification index $e(w \mid F)$ is the least $n > 0$ for which some nonzero $f \in F$ has $w$-order $n$ in $F'$. Assume tameness: for every place $w$ of $F'$ over $K$ the image of $e(w \mid F)$ in $K$ is nonzero. Writing $g = \dim_K H^1(0)$ for $F$ and $g' = \dim_K H^1(0)$ for $F'$, the adelic genera attached to the zero divisor (a divisor being a finitely supported integer-valued function on places), the conclusion is the equality of integers $$2g' - 2 = [F' : F]\,(2g - 2) + \sum_{w} \bigl(e(w \mid F) - 1\bigr),$$ the sum being taken, in the finitely supported sense, over all places $w$ of $F'$ over $K$.
--
--   This is the Hurwitz genus formula for a finite separable extension of function fields over an algebraically closed field, with the degree of the different computed by Dedekind's different theorem in the tame case, $d(w \mid v) = e(w \mid v) - 1$. It is used to compute the ramification sum for extensions of the rational function field and, in the modular setting, the genera of the function fields of modular curves of level $H$ and of $X_1$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_two_mul_genusFF_sub_two_eq_of_isSeparable_of_tame.lean

